  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 AM EST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nToday-Saturday...A reinforcing cold front will move across the \nFlorida peninsula today, bringing isolated light showers across \nthe Orlando metro and from Melbourne northward early this \nThanksgiving morning. Behind the front, drier air moves in as high\npressure develops across the southeast US, spreading towards \nFlorida through late week and into early weekend. Near-zero rain \nchances are forecast Friday and Saturday across east central \nFlorida, giving the area a much needed break from the rain. \nWesterly winds pick up this afternoon to 15 to 20 mph with gusts \nup to 30 mph possible at times. Less breezy into Friday with \noffshore winds around 10 to 15 mph. Saturday, as the high sits \ndirectly overhead, winds are forecast to become light and variable\nto even calm locally. Temperatures are anticipated to remain \ngenerally below normal through the remainder of the week, with \nhighs in the mid 60s to mid 70s today cooling into the low 60s to \nlow 70s Friday and Saturday. Noticeably cooler overnight with lows\nin the upper 40s to upper 50s.\n\nHazardous beach conditions are forecast to continue through \ntoday, with elevated seas and long period swells supporting a high\nrisk of rip currents and rough surf. Additionally, impacts from \nhigh astronomical tides are forecast to continue through this \nmorning's high tide cycle, resulting in minor flooding along the \ncoast. A Coastal Flood Advisory remains in effect through 11 AM. \nHeading to the beach and out into the ocean is not advised. Beach \nconditions are forecast to improve through the remainder of the \nweek as seas subside and the influence on the tides reduces. \nAlways heed the advice of lifeguards.\n\nSunday-Wednesday...Surface high pressure is kicked eastward out \nacross the Atlantic waters as a slow-moving cold front pushes \nacross the southeastern US late this weekend through early next \nweek. Moisture is anticipated to increase across the Florida \npeninsula ahead of the front, with isolated rain chances on Sunday\nincreasing in coverage on Monday. By Tuesday, an area of low \npressure appears to develop along the boundary in the Gulf and \naids in finally pushing the boundary through east central Florida \nas the low lifts across the peninsula, with the highest rain \nchances in the extended occurring Tuesday afternoon (50-70%). \nIsolated storm chances are forecast through the extended period \nwith activity, but confidence remains low at this time. Storm \nhazards will become clearer with time. By Wednesday, the cold \nfront is forecast to be south of east central Florida, with drier \nair settling across the local area. Temperatures remain generally \nnear normal areawide, trending warmer across the Treasure Coast \nand slightly above normal towards mid week. Overnight temperatures\nwill remain above normal.",
